  "content": {
    "id": "p45-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Use long division to show that $P(x)=x^3+2x^2-11x-12$ can be divided by $x-3$ with no remainder and hence express $P(x)$ as the product of three linear factors.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Quotient $x^2+5x+4$; remainder $0$. $P(x)=(x+4)(x+1)(x-3)$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{array}{r|l}&x^2+5x+4\\\\\\hline x-3&x^3+2x^2-11x-12\\\\&\\underline{-(x^3-3x^2)}\\\\&5x^2-11x-12\\\\&\\underline{-(5x^2-15x)}\\\\&4x-12\\\\&\\underline{-(4x-12)}\\\\&0\\end{array}$$\nThe remainder is $0$, so $P(x)$ is divisible by $x-3$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}x^2+5x+4&=(x+4)(x+1)\\\\P(x)&=(x-3)(x^2+5x+4)\\\\P(x)&=(x+4)(x+1)(x-3).\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 88
  },
